WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 85,

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.880
coming to you on May 30th, 2026.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:08.680
Let's look at what's new across the frontiers

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:09.920
of space and science.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.040
Blue Origins' new Glenn Test failure at Cape Canaveral

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.940
now threatens to push back NASA's Artemis crew

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.220
to lunar landing schedule.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.560
An explosion during testing at Cape Canaveral

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.060
has triggered immediate schedule reviews at NASA.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.020
Artemis lunar lander development depends

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.460
on Blue Origins' BE4 engines

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.060
and the new Glenn vehicle.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.940
Any extended stand-down could ripple

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.500
through both commercial and government payloads.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.960
Program managers are now assessing alternative paths

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.960
for the formal update expected within weeks.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.260
On a different note, United Launch Alliance

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.940
completed its second-to-last Atlas V flight

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.980
for Amazon's LEO constellation.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:55.980
The LEO Atlas 07 mission lifted off from pad 41

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.220
at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.400
At 7.53 p.m., Eastern Daylight Time.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.080
The rocket carried 29 satellites into low Earth orbit.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:07.480
The launch marks continued progress

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.000
toward an operational broadband constellation.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.680
The final Atlas V LEO mission is scheduled later this year.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.580
NASA's Maven spacecraft observed unexpected plasma

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.480
squeezing in the Martian atmosphere.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.820
The measurements show regions

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.120
where the solar wind interacts differently

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.420
than previously modeled.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.000
This finding refines understanding

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.860
of how Mars lost much of its early atmosphere.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.360
Researchers will compare the data

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.120
against future orbiter passes.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.340
Follow-up papers are expected

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.240
once the full data set is processed.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.800
The Maven findings on plasma squeezing at Mars

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.080
add a new layer to the long-running story

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.280
of atmospheric loss on the red planet.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.800
The orbiter's measurements show localized compression

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.840
that earlier models did not predict.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.700
Tightening constraints on how solar winds

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.160
strips ions over time.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.180
This directly informs the open question

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.660
of whether Mars ever held a thicker atmosphere

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.040
capable of supporting surface liquid water

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.380
for extended periods.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.220
Watch for the next Maven data release

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.040
and any coordinated observations

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.260
with the European Space Agency's Trace Gas Orbiter.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.820
You know what is fascinating about this?

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.600
If the Sun were replaced by a typical red dwarf,

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.760
Earth would orbit inside the star's habitable zone,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.920
yet face radiation levels hundreds of times higher

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.120
than we received today.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.720
Charged particles and intense ultraviolet flares

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.600
slam into the upper atmosphere,

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.060
heating it until gas molecules reach escape velocity.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.000
A single powerful flare can strip away

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.560
thousands of tons of atmosphere in hours

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.400
and many red dwarfs produce such events

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.780
several times per week.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.400
Over a billion years, this relentless bombardment

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.780
can reduce an Earth-like world to a thin,

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.160
remnant or bare rock.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.580
The exact balance between a planet's magnetic field

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.740
strength, its initial atmosphere mass

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.440
and the star's activity history remains unknown.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.400
Leaving astronomers unsure which worlds

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.760
might retain enough air for life to persist.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.900
Shifting to the moon, the Shenzhou 22 spacecraft

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.280
brought back the latest set of science samples

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.200
from the Tiangong Space Station.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.640
Experiments covered material science, fluid physics

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:20.460
and biology.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.640
Ground teams will now conduct follow-up analysis

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.080
on the return specimens.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.980
This marks the 10th such recovery mission

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.560
supporting station research.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.060
Additional sample returns are planned

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.320
through the end of the year.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.100
On the business side, seven companies were selected

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.560
for the Johnson Space Center

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.880
multiple award construction contract.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:43.760
The work covers up to $300 million

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.700
in facility upgrades, utilities and equipment improvements.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.060
All funds must be obligated by September 30th, 2026.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.720
The contract supports ongoing mission operations

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:56.920
at the Houston campus.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.840
Construction projects will begin once task orders are issued.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.460
Three members of the SpaceX crew 11 mission

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.420
will appear at NASA headquarters on June 1st.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:10.800
The event begins at 11 a.m. Eastern daylight time

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.340
in the web auditorium.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.700
It forms part of the crew's standard post-flight visit.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:18.480
Attendees can watch the discussion in person

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.540
or via NASA's live stream.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.760
The session offers direct insight

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.600
into the recent International Space Station expedition.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:28.980
In the evening sky, the constellation Bodets

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.180
and its bright star Arcturus form a kite-like shape

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:34.400
high in the east an hour after sunset.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.780
Viewers are encouraged to trace the kite-like shape

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:39.440
formed by nearby stars.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.740
Simple binoculars or the naked eye are sufficient.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.440
The guide also notes the double star new draconis

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:47.100
for splitting.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.640
A gathering in Sligo examined how Irish companies

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.680
can participate in space exploration supply chains.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.520
Discussions covered satellite services,

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.640
data applications and component manufacturing.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:04.160
Local firms heard from established European space contractors.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.460
The event aimed to position the region for future contracts.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.880
Follow-on workshops are expected later this year.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.980
The documentary Space Woman focuses on

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.320
Eileen Collins and our four space flights.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.620
The film covers her path from Air Force test pilot

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.620
to commanding STS-93 and STS-114.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.940
Archival footage and interviews highlight barriers

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:28.820
she navigated.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.760
The documentary is now screening at select venues.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.440
A wider releases plan for later in 2026.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:40.260
The latest manifest lists upcoming Falcon 9, Atlas V

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:42.700
and Starship attempts from Florida Pads.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:46.460
Several Starlink missions and a national security payload

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:47.620
are queued for June.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.280
Weather and range availability will determine final dates.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:55.040
The schedule is refreshed weekly on the newspapers site.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.340
Viewers can track changes through the coming month.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:02.540
A post notes that the Apollo 11 lunar module computer

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:04.640
contained only four kilobytes of RAM.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.620
The system still performed the power descent

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.420
and landing sequence successfully.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:13.140
Modern comparisons show how far on board processing

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.220
has advanced since 1969.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:17.800
Commenters discussed the trade-offs

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:21.320
between reliability and capability in early flight computers.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:23.840
The thread links to technical papers

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.600
on the Apollo guidance system.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.620
A visitor to the Adler Planetarium described

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.320
using the demonstration model

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.980
and now seeks a working personal version.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.880
The post explains the sphere's use

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.740
in modeling celestial coordinates.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.580
Responses pointed to museum replicas

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:42.160
and educational suppliers.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:44.540
No commercial full-scale versions

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.860
under a few thousand dollars were identified.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.660
The discussion continues in the comments.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:51.580
Next time, we will be watching

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.540
for the final Atlas VLEO mission

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.100
and any updates on new Glenn testing.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.320
That covers today's space and science news.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:02.840
Share this with a fellow space enthusiast

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:03.920
if you found it interesting.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.040
I'm Patrick in Vancouver, see you tomorrow.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:11.060
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:13.800
Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
